Subject: [PATCH 0012] Carve dtoa's Bigints on a pointer boundary
Upstream: CPython 3.13.7
Upstream-Archive: https://www.python.org/ftp/python/3.13.7/Python-3.13.7.tgz
Upstream-SHA256: 6c9d80839cfa20024f34d9a6dd31ae2a9cd97ff5e980e969209746037a5153b2
Build-Variants: capstone64 domain (musl)
Apply-From: upstream source root, with patch --batch --forward --fuzz=0 -p1

dtoa.c hands out small Bigints from a preallocated array of doubles in the
interpreter state (Balloc), in units of one double. A Bigint begins with a
`next` pointer. Where a pointer is a 16-byte capability it must be stored
16-aligned, and half of the Bigints carved at 8-byte steps were not: the domain
halted with "Unaligned cap access" (cause 4) in _PyDtoa_Fini, storing `next`
into a Bigint at _PyRuntime + 0x2a558 while returning it to the free list.

The array is declared with the alignment of a pointer, and Balloc rounds each
carve up to a multiple of _Alignof(Bigint) doubles. Where a pointer fits in a
double both are no-ops.

--- a/Include/internal/pycore_dtoa.h
+++ b/Include/internal/pycore_dtoa.h
@@ -48,7 +48,10 @@
     struct Bigint *p5s[Bigint_Pow5size];
     // XXX This should be freed during runtime fini.
     struct Bigint *freelist[Bigint_Kmax+1];
-    double preallocated[Bigint_PREALLOC_SIZE];
+    /* Bigints are carved out of this array and hold a pointer, so it is
+       aligned as a pointer is: more than a double where a pointer is a
+       capability, the same everywhere else. */
+    _Alignas(void *) double preallocated[Bigint_PREALLOC_SIZE];
     double *preallocated_next;
 };
 #define _dtoa_state_INIT(INTERP) \
--- a/Python/dtoa.c
+++ b/Python/dtoa.c
@@ -350,6 +350,11 @@
         x = 1 << k;
         len = (sizeof(Bigint) + (x-1)*sizeof(ULong) + sizeof(double) - 1)
             /sizeof(double);
+        /* Keep every Bigint carved from private_mem aligned for its `next`
+           pointer. Where a pointer is a 16-byte capability, a Bigint 8 bytes
+           off faulted on the first store of `next` (Bfree, at finalization);
+           everywhere else a pointer fits a double and this does nothing. */
+        len = _Py_SIZE_ROUND_UP(len, _Alignof(Bigint) / sizeof(double));
         if (k <= Bigint_Kmax &&
             pmem_next - private_mem + len <= (Py_ssize_t)Bigint_PREALLOC_SIZE
         ) {
